// A type's abstractions are bound while the type is name resolved -- each one's
// default methods join the type's dictionary before any of its method bodies is
// resolved -- so what can go wrong in reaching them is reported at this stage:
// two types that each stand on the other, so neither can be completed first.
//
// A trait's field arriving under a name the type already declares is no longer
// one of them. A trait contributes no fields: what its fields state is a
// requirement the type satisfies by declaring them itself, so declaring the name
// is what compliance is. The splice survives only between an enum and its
// variants, where enum-nameres-splice pins the duplicate.
//
// Name-resolution stage only: name resolution returns before type checking, so
// nothing about signatures or layout belongs here. Those are in
// trait-typecheck-conform.

// Two traits that each name the other. The first declared is resolved first
// and demands the second, which demands the first back and finds it still under
// way, so the second is refused where it names the first. The compiler used to
// loop here without end.
trait Loop is Back {
  a i32;
}

trait Back is Loop {  //~ ErrorCircular:15 "each depends on the other"
  b i32;
}

// The shortest cycle, through a mixin: a trait mixing itself in
trait Ring {
  mixin Ring;  //~ ErrorCircular:9 "each depends on the other"
  r i32;
}
